VIM4 boot randomness (offline candidate)
=======================================

ft_board_setup creates /chosen/kaslr-seed (one big-endian u64) and rng-seed
(32 opaque bytes) from separate T7 RNG samples. The CFG at 0xfe440788 and
OUT0 at 0xfe440790 follow arch/arm/mach-meson/t7/hw_rng.c: wait for
READY (0xfe440780) bit 0, wait for CFG bit 0, start CFG bit 31, wait for it
to clear, read OUT0 and acknowledge READY with 1. The original d1c2033
copied the Linux handshake and omitted READY arbitration/acknowledgement.
Polls are bounded to 10 ms per status bit; a non-returning bus transaction
cannot be bounded by a software loop. No seed value is printed, persisted to the environment, or
derived from a timer. Obvious stuck output is rejected, but these checks
are not an entropy certification.

The older CONFIG_AML_KASLR_SEED boot_jump path is disabled for VIM4: it
requires a pre-existing property, widens only one 32-bit rand() result and
writes native-endian bytes. It must not overwrite this handoff. The kernel
still honors an explicit nokaslr command line; the old implicit ramdump
environment/ramoops_io_en policy is no longer applied. Set nokaslr explicitly
when debugging requires stable kernel addresses.

Old seed properties are removed before each attempt. Timeout, stuck output,
or insufficient FDT space leaves both absent and reports a numeric error;
boot continues without this optional handoff. There is no fallback PRNG.
Linux 7.3 consumes/clears these properties: a missing live rng-seed property
alone is not evidence that firmware failed to supply it.

Run python3 tools/test-vim4-random-seed.py, tools/test-vim4-dtb-load.py and
the existing PD handoff test.
The host test compiles the actual functions and libfdt, with mocked MMIO,
covering READY/CFG/OUT0/ACK ordering, fresh FDTs, retries, byte order, all
three bounded polling faults and allocation
rollback. Build and repack as documented in VIM4.md using private O= and
BL33Z BUILD_BASE directories. No board flash or boot test is implied.

The d54877c9f5 combined candidate reset repeatedly during early reserved-DTB
loading, before ft_board_setup or RNG access. This is not evidence of an RNG
bus fault. Linux VIM4 now loads its filesystem DTB directly and rejects the
common raw reserved-DTB fallback, which can otherwise repair Android copies
inside an unrelated Linux filesystem. The physical reset mechanism remains
unconfirmed; this change removes that path, not a proven watchdog diagnosis.
The signed BL31 blob is unchanged; no matching BL31 source/exception trace
was available to establish an EL3 reset cause.

Before any eMMC flash, first boot the candidate from removable microSD and
verify PD, actual HID and seeds across cold boot and two normal reboots.
Remove the card and cold power-cycle to return to the retained eMMC firmware.
Compare every non-BL33 device-FIP
payload against the deployed pd-handoff image and retain its full prefix
backup. The existing SD/eMMC procedure must preserve sector-zero bytes
442..511 and the partition layout. A boot failure can require physical SPI
OOWOW recovery. FUNCTION+RESET relies on the eMMC U-Boot reaching its key
check; with this early failure, remove SD and hold FUNCTION during cold
power-on instead. This candidate is not suitable for unattended flashing.
